# Network phase N5: `ntp` sets the clock from a time server (here: a fake one
# whose clock started at 2030-01-02T03:04:05Z).
@net
@ntp
$ net dhcp
~ ^address 10\.0\.2\.15/24 \(DHCP\)
$ date
!~ ^2030
$ ntp {ntp}
~ ^clock set to 2030-01-02T03:0[45]:\d\dZ \(it was .* behind; server stratum 2\)$
$ date
~ ^2030-01-02T03:0[45]:\d\dZ$
# Asking again: the clock now agrees with the server.
$ ntp {ntp}
~ ^clock set to 2030-01-02T03:0[45]:\d\dZ \(it was right to within a second; server stratum 2\)$
# The event log keeps time with the new clock.
$ log 3
~ 2030-01-02T03:0[45]:\d\dZ.*ntp
# A server that does not know the time changes nothing.
$ ntp {ntp-unsynced}
~ ^10\.0\.2\.2: the server does not know the time itself$
$ date
~ ^2030-01-02T03:0[45]:\d\dZ$
$ uptime
~ ^up 0m \d\ds$
$ ntp host:port
~ ^usage: ntp \[SERVER\[:PORT\]\]$
